    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">The data that I am simulating is a mixture of Asymmetric hyperbolic distributions (laplace is a special case). This distribution can be generated as:<div>w ~ gamma(1,alpha0)</div><div>y ~ N(w*a,w*sigma^2) </div><div><br></div><div>where a and sigma2 are constants.</div><div><br><div><br></div><div>So I generate the mixture as such:<div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sample</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">prob </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> p</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  w </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rgamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">alpha0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> muk</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]+</span><span style=\"color:#000\">w</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ak</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]+</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sqrt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">w</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma2k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]])*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><div>muk is a location parameter.<br><br></div><div>And the corresponding stan segment is as follows:</div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    w</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> gamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2.0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\">//alpha0 = 2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>        acc</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">theta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">])</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal_log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]+</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">w</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sqrt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">w</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">])*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">increment_log_prob</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">log_sum_exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">acc</span><span style=\"color:#660\">));</span></font><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></code></div><div><br></div></div></div></div><div><br></div><div>The question is:</div><div>1. Do I need to declare a w[t] for each k? (and why? i.e. does it need to be w[t,k]~gamma(2.0,1.0))</div><div>2. Can I speed this stan code by declaring w ~ gamma(2.0,1.0) outside the for loop</div><div>3. Does stan care if I use a float number 2.0 or can I write 2 inside that gamma()</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "tj1dHSOdBAAJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2016-04-17T19:32:30Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Mixture of Asymmetric  Laplace distributions",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Apr 17, 2016, at 1:55 AM, Sachinthaka Abeywardana &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">sachin.ab...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; The data that I am simulating is a mixture of Asymmetric hyperbolic distributions (laplace is a special case). This distribution can be generated as:\r<br>&gt; w ~ gamma(1,alpha0)\r<br>&gt; y ~ N(w*a,w*sigma^2) \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; where a and sigma2 are constants.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; So I generate the mixture as such:\r<br>&gt; for (t in 2:T){\r<br>&gt;   z[t] = <a href=\"http://sample.int\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://sample.int&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792461794545000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1K6CaZXL8W8KHMBCrX2bMH\">sample.int</a>(K,1,prob = p)\r<br>&gt;   w = rgamma(1,alpha0)\r<br>&gt;   y[t] = muk[z[t]]+w*ak[z[t]]+sqrt(w*sigma2k[z[t]])*rnorm(1)\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; muk is a location parameter.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; And the corresponding stan segment is as follows:\r<br>&gt; for (t in 2:N) {\r<br>&gt;     w[t] ~ gamma(2.0,1.0); //alpha0 = 2\r<br>&gt;     for (k in 1:K)\r<br>&gt;         acc[k] &lt;- log(theta[k]) + normal_log(y[t],mu[k]+a[k]*w[t],sqrt(w[t])*sigma[k]);\r<br>&gt;     increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(acc));\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; The question is:\r<br>&gt; 1. Do I need to declare a w[t] for each k? (and why? i.e. does it need to be w[t,k]~gamma(2.0,1.0))\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Those are different models.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; 2. Can I speed this stan code by declaring w ~ gamma(2.0,1.0) outside the for loop\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "You need to write to_vector(w) ~ gamma(2, 1);   And it&#39;ll be more efficient\r<br>than looping (not because of loop, but because of reduced virtual function\r<br>calls during derivative calculations).\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; 3. Does stan care if I use a float number 2.0 or can I write 2 inside that gamma()\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Integers are automatically promoted to reals for function\r<br>arguments requiring real arguments and in arithmetic operators\r<br>when the other operand is real (e.g., 1 / 2.0 will evaluate to\r<br>0.5).\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
